    "A hash map works, but it ignores the fact that the list is sorted and costs O(n) memory.",
    "Start one pointer at each end. If their sum is too small, which pointer can usefully move?",
    "While left < right: if the sum is below target, left++; above target, right--; equal, return [left + 1, right + 1]."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function findParcelPair(weights, target) {\n  let left = 0\n  let right = weights.length - 1\n  while (left < right) {\n    const sum = weights[left] + weights[right]\n    if (sum === target) return [left + 1, right + 1]\n    if (sum < target) left++\n    else right--\n  }\n  return []\n}\n",
    "py": "def find_parcel_pair(weights, target):\n    left, right = 0, len(weights) - 1\n    while left < right:\n        total = weights[left] + weights[right]\n        if total == target:\n            return [left + 1, right + 1]\n        if total < target:\n            left += 1\n        else:\n            right -= 1\n    return []\n",
    "explanation": "If the sum is too small, weights[left] cannot pair with anything at or left of right, because those are all no larger, so left can be discarded. Symmetrically a too-large sum discards right. Each step removes one candidate without ever skipping the answer, so one pass suffices."
  },
